    "openrouter_cost_usd": 0.085852,
    "reply": "The retrieved records cover EGFR-directed clinical studies in NSCLC and a set of ClinVar EGFR sequence variants, but they do not report pathogenicity or recruitment status, so they only partly answer the question [1]. Several studies focus on EGFR tyrosine kinase inhibitors, including osimertinib [3].\n\n## EGFR-TKI Studies\n\nOne study is A Retrospective Study of EGFR-TKIs,Gefitinib, Erlotinib and Osimertinib in NSCLC Patients Treatment [1]. Another is Survival Outcome of EGFR-TKI in Uncommon EGFR Mutant Advanced NSCLC [2]. A third is Osimertinib for Advanced EGFR-positive NSCLC Patients [3]. None of these trial records states whether it is currently recruiting [3].\n\n## CNS and Cellular Therapy\n\nOne study is CSF Analysis in EGFR Mutant Non-Small Cell Lung Cancer With Leptomeningeal Disease [4]. Another study is EGFR/B7H3 CAR-T on Lung Cancer and Triple Negative Breast Cancer [5]. This shows EGFR is being studied as a target beyond kinase inhibition [5].\n\n## ClinVar EGFR Variants\n\nThe ClinVar variants are named on the NM_005228.5 transcript and span the extracellular and kinase regions [6]. One example is NM_005228.5(EGFR):c.840C>A (p.Asn280Lys) [6]. Another is NM_005228.5(EGFR):c.2339T>C (p.Ile780Thr) [17]. A third is NM_005228.5(EGFR):c.2741C>G (p.Pro914Arg) [15]. These variant records do not include clinical significance or links to NSCLC [6].",
